course: dictionary
lesson: 8268
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning is right. The word rifle can name a gun, describe a way of searching, or describe a very hard kick in football. «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall» is about guns, because armed and attack are the clues.
explain: Look at the words near the word. «The burglar rifled through the drawers.» Here rifled is a verb meaning searched quickly and roughly, often to steal. The burglar and the drawers tell you it is not about a gun.
explain: In sport, «He rifled the ball into the net» means he hit the ball very hard and straight. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense. Hit the ball very hard and straight: yes, that fits.
explain: «Mole comes back with an armful of rifles.» Here rifles is a noun, a name for things: long guns. A rifle is a noun when it names something, and a verb when it describes an action, such as rifle, rifled, rifling.
fact: A rifle is a long gun fired from the shoulder.
fact: Inside the barrel of a rifle there are spiral grooves, so the bullet spins and flies straight.
fact: As a verb, «rifled» means searched quickly and roughly, often to steal.
fact: In sport, «rifled» means hit very hard and straight.
fact: Real guns are never toys. If you find one, do not touch it: leave the place and tell an adult straight away.
exercise: «The burglar rifled through the drawers.» What does rifled mean here? | Cleaned with a soft cloth | Hung up neatly in a row | Painted in bright colours | *Searched quickly and roughly, often to steal
exercise: «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall.» What is a rifle in this sentence? | *A long gun fired from the shoulder | A small wooden boat | A kind of spade for digging | A type of hat
exercise: «He rifled the ball into the net.» What does rifled mean in football? | Kicked it over the fence | *Hit it very hard and straight | Passed it slowly to a friend | Dropped it gently
test: «Mole comes back with an armful of rifles.» What kind of word is rifles here? | An adjective: it describes a thing | An adverb: it tells how something is done | *A noun: it names things, long guns | A verb: it describes an action
test: You find a rifle in a shed. What should you do? | Hide it under the bed | *Leave it alone and tell an adult straight away | Fire it at a tin to test it | Pick it up to see how heavy it is
exam: Which words in «The burglar rifled through the drawers» show that rifled means searching? | The | The sentence is short | *Through the drawers | The burglar
